1) Class Responsibilities and Relationships

Shell: serves as the interactive command-line driver. It holds the source Image,
the current charset, resolution,
rounding mode, and output target. It reads user commands, validates them,
and dispatches to private handler methods (doAdd, doRes, etc.),
using UserInputException to report any invalid input without terminating the session.

AsciiArtAlgorithm: encapsulates a single run of the conversion pipeline.
It pads the source image to powers of two, divides it into square tiles,
computes each tile’s normalized brightness, and invokes a provided
SubImgCharMatcher to choose the best ASCII character per tile.
Its run() method returns the resulting 2D char matrix.

UserInputException: is a checked exception thrown by Shell whenever a
command or its arguments fail validation;
the main loop catches it and prints its message.

KeyboardInput: is a simple singleton wrapper around Scanner that provides
trimmed line-reads from standard input for Shell.

AsciiOutput: is an interface defining a single out(char[][]) method.
ConsoleAsciiOutput and HtmlAsciiOutput implement this interface to render the
ASCII art to the console or to an HTML file, respectively.

Image: wraps a 2D Color[][] pixel array, loaded from disk or provided manually,
and offers getters for width, height, individual pixels, and an optional saveImage() method.

SubImgCharMatcher: builds and caches the raw brightness of each ASCII character (via CharConverter),
normalizes these values to [0,1], and provides three lookup methods
(closest, closestGE, closestLE) to match a target brightness, breaking ties by the lowest ASCII code.

CharConverter: renders a single character in “Courier New” at 16×16 pixels,
 producing a black-and-white mask (boolean[][]) that SubImgCharMatcher uses to compute raw brightness.



2) Data Structures, Usage, and Complexity

A HashSet<Character> in Shell stores the active charset,
providing O(1) add/remove/contains for up to 95 printable ASCII characters.

An ArrayList<Character> in doChars collects and sorts
the charset in O(n log n) time—acceptable for n≤95—and prints it.

Two HashMap<Character,Double> instances in SubImgCharMatcher hold cached raw brightness
(RAW_CACHE) and current normalized brightness (normBrightness), both offering O(1) lookups and updates.

Primitive 2D arrays (double[][] brightnessCache and char[][] ascii) in AsciiArtAlgorithm
are sized by the padded image’s tile grid and accessed in O(1) per element;
initializing and scanning them is linear in the number of tiles.

Arrays.fill(...) initializes the brightness cache in O(rows×cols).
Overall, this design yields constant-time brightness lookups and linear-time image scans,
with memory proportional to the charset size and the number of image tiles.



3) Exception Handling for User Input
Each doXXX(...) handler in Shell declares throws UserInputException.
They first verify parts.length and argument formats,
then throw a UserInputException with a specific message
(e.g., "Did not add due to incorrect format.") on any violation.
The main loop in run(...) catches every UserInputException and prints its message exactly once,
then continues prompting the user without terminating.

4) SubImgCharMatcher API Changes
I extended the original API for two key reasons:

Support for “round up” and “round down” modes
To cleanly implement the round up and round down options without duplicating complex loops in
AsciiArtAlgorithm or Shell, I added two new public methods:
public char closestGE(double target);
char closestLE(double target);
This way all brightness‐rounding logic stays centralized in the matcher, and AsciiArtAlgorithm simply
chooses between closestGE, closestLE or the original closest.

Improved single‐responsibility and convenience

I introduced public char closest(double target) as a clear entry point for absolute rounding
(internally calls getCharByImageBrightness).

I added public void clear() to support the “remove all” command in Shell
without exposing or iterating over the internal map.

5) Changes to Supplied Classes

No modifications were made to the ascii_output package (AsciiOutput, ConsoleAsciiOutput, HtmlAsciiOutput).
All modifications to the provided classes were purely cosmetic—formatting, Javadoc comments,
and naming conventions—without any functional changes.